==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ang|hyċġġan|hyċġean|hiċġan|hyġġean|hyċċan}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*huggjan}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*hugjaną|t=think, consider}}. Cognate with {{cog|osx|huggian}}, {{cog|goh|huggen}}, {{cog|non|hyggja}}, {{cog|got|𐌷𐌿𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|hyċġan}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|hyċġan}}

# to [[think]]
#* {{quote-book|ang|text=''…þæt biþ in eorle · indryhten þēaw, <br/>þæt hē his ferðlocan · fæste binde,<br/>healde his hordcōfan; · '''hyċġe''' swā hē wille.''|t=…that a noble habit is in a brave man,<br/> that he '''would''' tightly bind his spirit,<br/> keep his treasure-chamber; '''think''' as he want.|title=The Wanderer|year=10th century}}
# to think about, to think of, to [[consider]]
#* [https://web.archive.org/web/20180629051659/http://faculty.virginia.edu/OldEnglish/aspr/a3.23.html "The Wife's Lament"]
#*: {{quote|ang|Ongunnon þæt þæs mannes māgas '''hyċġan''' þurh dierne ġeþōht þæt hīe tōdǣlden unc.|The person's relatives began '''to think of''' a secret plan to separate us.}}

====Usage notes====
The conjugation table below shows the inherited forms of ''hyċġan'', with ''hy(ċ)ġ-'' throughout the present tense and ''hogd-'' throughout the past. These are the normal forms in Early West Saxon. In the other dialects and Late West Saxon, it was often inflected as a weak class II verb ''hogian''.

